WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.168
A fraud check loads the reference in a merchant object, and follows it to a risk score.

2
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.054
To empty this region, the collector copies the risk score out.

3
00:00:12.054 --> 00:00:14.707
But the merchant still holds the old address.

4
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:21.840
So the usual collector stops every thread, copies, fixes every reference, and only then lets them run.

5
00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:28.744
In lesson 4's G1 log, that pause took 16 ms, almost all of it copying.

6
00:00:28.744 --> 00:00:37.165
On a 32 GB heap, a full GC can stop for seconds: an outage for a fraud check with a 20 ms budget.

7
00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:41.457
How could the collector move objects while the threads keep running?

8
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:45.599
ZGC adds two things.

9
00:00:45.599 --> 00:00:50.291
Every reference in the heap carries a few extra bits: its color.

10
00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:54.554
After every reference load, the JIT adds a check: the load barrier.

11
00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:58.885
It compares that color with the one the collector calls good right now.

12
00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:02.124
Nearly always they match: a test and a branch.

13
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:08.057
Now the collector changes the good color, and copies the risk score while the thread runs on.

14
00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:12.125
The next load sees an old color, so the barrier takes the slow path.

15
00:01:12.125 --> 00:01:16.664
It finds the new address, and writes the fixed reference back into the merchant.

16
00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:19.177
The load after that is fast again.

17
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:34.513
So ZGC stops threads only to handle their roots: three pauses per collection, about 0.02 ms each in the demo, while the 0.052 s collection runs beside them.

18
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:39.827
The price: 8-byte references, so a 40-byte Payment takes 48.

19
00:01:39.827 --> 00:01:42.278
CPU for GC threads and barriers.

20
00:01:42.278 --> 00:01:45.493
And headroom, or allocating threads stall.

21
00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:48.153
Most services stay on G1.

22
00:01:48.153 --> 00:01:53.714
Pick ZGC when pauses must stay under about 10 ms, and cores are spare.

23
00:01:53.714 --> 00:01:56.256
Shenandoah makes the same trade.

24
00:01:56.433 --> 00:02:02.840
Serial fits 1 CPU, Parallel fits batch jobs, and Epsilon is only for tests.

25
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:13.092
The rule: ZGC moves objects while your threads run, because a barrier on every reference load fixes stale references.

26
00:02:13.092 --> 00:02:15.053
You pay in CPU and memory.

27
00:02:15.233 --> 00:02:15.956
Quick check.

28
00:02:15.956 --> 00:02:18.606
The barrier just fixed the merchant's field.

29
00:02:18.606 --> 00:02:20.352
What does the next load cost?

30
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:24.233
A test and a branch.

31
00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:26.032
Its color is good again.
